1. Create a dedicated role for the service account.
2. Grant that role access to only those 2 tables.
3. Deny SELECT on those 2 tables to db_datareader and other broad read roles.
4. Make sure the service account is not inside the denied broad group.
5. Remove developers from sysadmin if they should not access the tables.



1. Confirm service account is in your restricted role
USE [M4];
GO

ALTER ROLE [Your_Restricted_Role_Name]
ADD MEMBER [DOMAIN\ServiceAccountName];
GO
2. Grant the service account role access to the 2 tables
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE 
ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table1]
TO [Your_Restricted_Role_Name];
GO

GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE 
ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table2]
TO [Your_Restricted_Role_Name];
GO

If the service account only needs read access, use this instead:

GRANT SELECT 
ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table1]
TO [Your_Restricted_Role_Name];
GO

GRANT SELECT 
ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table2]
TO [Your_Restricted_Role_Name];
GO
3. Deny broad readers from those 2 tables
DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table1] TO [db_datareader];
GO

DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table2] TO [db_datareader];
GO

If you have custom read-only roles, deny them too:

DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table1] TO [Custom_ReadOnly_Role];
GO

DENY SELECT ON OBJECT::[SchemaName].[Table2] TO [Custom_ReadOnly_Role];
GO
4. Validate permissions
SELECT 
    USER_NAME(p.grantee_principal_id) AS PrincipalName,
    p.state_desc,
    p.permission_name,
    SCHEMA_NAME(o.schema_id) AS SchemaName,
    o.name AS ObjectName
FROM sys.database_permissions p
JOIN sys.objects o
    ON p.major_id = o.object_id
WHERE o.name IN ('Table1', 'Table2')
ORDER BY ObjectName, PrincipalName;
